Description: Converts a map of regions, as an 'sf' object, into a map of
    equally sized hexagons with one hexagon for each region. Regions are
    allocated hexagons by minimising the total squared displacement of all
    regions, so regions keep their relative positions, while distant islands
    are tiled separately. Groups of regions (such as states) can be kept
    together using one of several layouts, including a diffusion cartogram
    (Gastner and Newman, 2004) <doi:10.1073/pnas.0400280101> and spatially
    ordered treemaps (Wood and Dykes, 2008) <doi:10.1109/TVCG.2008.165>. This
    makes small but important regions as visible as large, sparsely populated
    ones.
